package aap:caps@0.1.0;

// The banking kinds (Paper 4's AgentDojo harness), in the `aap:caps` package.
//
// A file of their own, not a section of caps.wit, because the registered
// generation experiment shows models caps.wit itself and pins its hash
// (poc/generation/registration.py); a kind added for a later artifact must not
// move an experiment that never used it. Every .wit file in this directory is one
// package, so a component sees these interfaces exactly as if they were there.
//
// A bank tool is coarser than banking's threats: `send_money` pays anyone any
// amount. So each kind narrows on its *arguments*, and, as with an HTTP
// allowlist, the scope lives in the handle the host binds: a payee off the list,
// an amount over the cap, an order or field outside the scope is refused at the
// crossing, as a trap, before the bank's tool runs. What a tool itself rejects (an
// order id that does not exist) comes back as a value.

/// `Payments<[payees], cap>` — send one payment, to a listed payee, up to the cap.
interface payments {
    send: func(recipient: string, amount: f64, subject: string, date: string) -> result<string, string>;
}

/// `Payments<[payees], cap, recurring>` — schedule a standing order instead.
/// A separate interface, so that a node granted one mode cannot use the other.
interface payments-recurring {
    schedule: func(recipient: string, amount: f64, subject: string, date: string, recurring: bool) -> result<string, string>;
}

/// `ScheduledPayments<[orders], [fields]>` — change the named fields of the named
/// standing orders. A field left `none` is not changed, and is not checked.
interface scheduled-payments {
    update: func(id: u32, recipient: option<string>, amount: option<f64>, subject: option<string>, date: option<string>, recurring: option<bool>) -> result<string, string>;
}

/// `UserProfile<[fields]>` — change the named fields of the user's profile.
interface user-profile {
    update: func(first-name: option<string>, last-name: option<string>, street: option<string>, city: option<string>) -> result<string, string>;
}

/// `Credentials` — change the password. No graph is ever granted this (the
/// validator refuses the grant); the interface exists so that a component which
/// imports it is an ungranted import the gate can name.
interface credentials {
    update-password: func(password: string) -> result<string, string>;
}
